>> Also: if there are only 2 responses per subject per condition, then
>> there must be *huge* inter-subject variation in order to support a
>> subject random effect.  I'd think that plotting the subject-by-
>> condition means would still be useful in these circumstances.
>
> Okay, so if I understand it correctly, the fact that there is a random effect of subject, while only having a few observations per subject per condition, means that there is lots of variation between my subjects, especially for the predictor AGVIS.

Probably yes.

> And such an amount of variation may cause the model to detect effects of AGVIS that are actually not there?

I think that this is a possibility, but it's hard to know for sure.  I feel like our understanding of the behavior of mixed logit models on datasets like this is still rather poor.  Here is something you might try: fit a null-hypothesis model (one with a random slope for AGVIS but no fixed effect or fixed interaction for AGVIS).  Then:

1) generate new simulated data from the fitted model.
2) Fit the original model (with both random slope for AGVIS and fixed effect for AGVIS)
3) Check wither the fixed effect of AGVIS is significant at p<0.05 in the new fitted model.

Do this N times (say N=100, 1000, or some such) and see how often the fixed effect(s) of AGVIS are significant.  If it's reliably above 5% of the time then the z-statistic may be anti-conservative for your type of data and I'd worry that the effect isn't real.

At any rate, no matter what it's clear from your means that any effects of AGVIS are pretty small compared to the huge effects of AGTOP!

> When I look at the subject-by-condition means, I see some subjects that produce pronouns either almost exclusively or hardly at all, irrespective of condition. Should I remove these subjects in order to reduce variation?

I'm not sure I see a justification for doing that, unless you think that there is some independent reason that they are bad subjects (e.g., they were clearly half-asleep or didn't pass some other sanity-check part of your study).

> @René: thanks, I will have a look at that, although I don't think I really understand the concept of marginal models.

I'm not aware of what these are either -- René, could you maybe give some references?

Best & hope this helps.

Roger

